A table stores the smallest possible maximum load for the first `i` books and `j` students. To fill a cell, try every possible place where the last student could start.

The prefix sum array makes the pages in any section quick to calculate. This does not change `nums`.

```python
class Solution:
    def findPages(self, nums, m):
        n = len(nums)
        if n == 0 or m > n:
            return -1

        prefix = [0] * (n + 1)
        for i in range(n):
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i]

        inf = float('inf')
        dp = [[inf] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]

        for books in range(1, n + 1):
            dp[1][books] = prefix[books]

        for students in range(2, m + 1):
            for books in range(students, n + 1):
                for split in range(students - 1, books):
                    last_group = prefix[books] - prefix[split]
                    worst = max(dp[students - 1][split], last_group)
                    dp[students][books] = min(dp[students][books], worst)

        return dp[m][n]
```

For one student, the answer is simply the sum of all pages. For more students, each split compares the earlier groups with the last group and keeps the smaller maximum.

Time complexity is `O(m * n^2)`. The table uses `O(m * n)` space.

This version treats `index` as the next book to assign. It tries each possible ending point for the current student and remembers results for repeated states.

The memo table is useful because many different earlier choices can reach the same book index and student count. If there are more students than books, the required split is impossible.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] pages;
    private int[][] prefix;
    private Integer[][] memo;

    public int findPages(int[] nums, int m) {
        if (nums.length == 0 || m > nums.length) {
            return -1;
        }

        pages = nums;
        int n = nums.length;
        prefix = new int[1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[0][i + 1] = prefix[0][i] + nums[i];
        }

        memo = new Integer[n][m + 1];
        return split(0, m);
    }

    private int split(int index, int studentsLeft) {
        int n = pages.length;
        if (studentsLeft == 1) {
            return prefix[0][n] - prefix[0][index];
        }
        if (memo[index][studentsLeft] != null) {
            return memo[index][studentsLeft];
        }

        int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int currentPages = 0;

        for (int end = index; end <= n - studentsLeft; end++) {
            currentPages += pages[end];
            int remaining = split(end + 1, studentsLeft - 1);
            best = Math.min(best, Math.max(currentPages, remaining));
        }

        memo[index][studentsLeft] = best;
        return best;
    }
}
```

A small dry run with `[10, 20, 30]` and two students checks cuts after the first and second book. The better result is `30`, from `[10, 20]` and `[30]`.

The running time is `O(m * n^2)`, with `O(m * n)` memo space. The input array stays unchanged.

Here the possible answer range starts at the largest single book and ends at the total pages. For each middle value, a greedy scan counts how many students are needed.

If the scan needs at most `m` students, the limit can be lowered. Otherwise it must be raised. The input slice is only read.

```go
func findPages(nums []int, m int) int {
	if len(nums) == 0 || m > len(nums) {
		return -1
	}

	low := 0
	high := 0
	for _, pages := range nums {
		if pages > low {
			low = pages
		}
		high += pages
	}

	for low < high {
		limit := low + (high-low)/2
		students := 1
		current := 0

		for _, pages := range nums {
			if current+pages > limit {
				students++
				current = pages
			} else {
				current += pages
			}
		}

		if students <= m {
			high = limit
		} else {
			low = limit + 1
		}
	}

	return low
}
```

For `[5, 10, 20]` and two students, limits below 20 fail because one student must receive the 20-page book. The search never goes below the largest book.

Both the scan and the binary search use small extra storage. The time complexity is `O(n log(sum(nums)))`, and the space complexity is `O(1)`.

The answer is the largest page count given to one student. I binary search that value and check if the books can be split without crossing it.

Books must stay in their original order. When adding the next book would go over the limit, the code starts a new student.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findPages(vector<int> &nums, int m) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (m > n || n == 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        long long low = 0;
        long long high = 0;
        for (int pages : nums) {
            low = max(low, (long long)pages);
            high += pages;
        }

        long long answer = high;
        while (low <= high) {
            long long limit = low + (high - low) / 2;
            int students = 1;
            long long currentPages = 0;

            for (int pages : nums) {
                if (currentPages + pages > limit) {
                    students++;
                    currentPages = pages;
                } else {
                    currentPages += pages;
                }
            }

            if (students <= m) {
                answer = limit;
                high = limit - 1;
            } else {
                low = limit + 1;
            }
        }

        return (int)answer;
    }
};
```

For `[12, 34, 67, 90]` and `m = 2`, a limit of 113 makes groups `[12, 34, 67]` and `[90]`, so it is possible. Smaller limits are checked until the minimum valid one is found.

Time complexity is `O(n log(sum(nums)))`, and extra space is `O(1)`.

The recursive function chooses a continuous group for the current student, then solves the remaining books for the remaining students. It checks every valid cut, so the code is easy to follow but not suitable for very large limits.

A single book per student is allowed. The first `m - 1` students are kept with at least one book, leaving the rest for the last student.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int findPages(List<int> nums, int m)  {
        if (nums == null || nums.Count == 0 || m > nums.Count) {
            return -1;
        }

        return Search(nums, 0, m);
    }

    private int Search(List<int> nums, int start, int studentsLeft) {
        int n = nums.Count;
        if (studentsLeft == 1) {
            int total = 0;
            for (int i = start; i < n; i++) {
                total += nums[i];
            }
            return total;
        }

        int best = int.MaxValue;
        int current = 0;

        for (int end = start; end <= n - studentsLeft; end++) {
            current += nums[end];
            int rest = Search(nums, end + 1, studentsLeft - 1);
            best = Math.Min(best, Math.Max(current, rest));
        }

        return best;
    }
}
```

The main edge cases are handled before recursion: an empty list has no answer, and assigning more students than books is not possible. Since every split is explored, the trade-off is high running time.

Time complexity is exponential in the number of books in the worst case. The recursion uses `O(m)` stack space.

